# Pre-Deletion Validation

Before deleting the volumes, the server-side mount points were checked.

### Check mounted filesystems

```bash
df -h
```

### Identify the mounted devices

```bash
lsblk
```

Example:

```bash
lsblk -f
```

This was used to identify which EBS device was associated with:

```text
/apps
/loggers
swap
```

### Check filesystem utilization

```bash
df -h /apps
df -h /loggers
```

### Check swap

```bash
swapon --show
```

Also verify:

```bash
free -h
```

---

# Backup to S3

Before removing the EBS volumes, the required data from both servers was copied to an S3 bucket.

The backup was performed to ensure that the data could be recovered if required after volume deletion.

Example validation:

```bash
aws s3 ls s3://<bucket-name>/
```

For application data:

```bash
aws s3 ls s3://<bucket-name>/SNF-1/
aws s3 ls s3://<bucket-name>/SNF-2/
```

The actual bucket name should be kept according to the organization's internal configuration.

---

# Backup Validation

Before proceeding with deletion, the S3 backup was cross-checked to confirm that the required files were available.

Validation included:

```bash
aws s3 ls s3://<bucket-name>/SNF-1/ --recursive
```

and:

```bash
aws s3 ls s3://<bucket-name>/SNF-2/ --recursive
```

The purpose was to ensure that the required data had been successfully copied before removing the EBS volumes.

